Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that average utilitarianism is more justifiable to rational agents compared to classical utilitarianism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a prominent advantage that average utilitarianism has over classical utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The main feature of classical utilitarianism that makes it less justifiable to the rational agent than average utilitarianism is the population size problem. Under classical utilitarianism, adding more people below the average utility increases overall welfare. In fact, a society with a massive amount of miserable people with just-above-zero utility could be better, in the eyes of the classical utilitarian, than a world with 100 people of substantially higher utility. Also, from the original position, the result of the principle of insufficient reason mathematical derivation is exactly the same as the utility predicted through average utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Still, the difference principle is better than average utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A citizen is whoever is entitled to share in deliberative or judicial office. The definition is functional rather than legal, geographic, or genealogical, and since regimes differ in kind, a citizen in a democracy may not be one in an oligarchy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle’s answer to “who is a citizen?”, prior to defining the city, since the city is a multitude of citizens.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>He rules out three rival definitions:</p><ul><li><strong>Residence</strong>, since metics and slaves share the citizens’ domicile.</li><li><strong>Legal standing</strong>, since parties to a commercial treaty can also sue and be sued.</li><li><strong>Descent</strong>, which cannot cover a city’s founders or later enfranchisements.</li></ul><p><em>Office</em> is stretched to include the juror and assemblyman, who hold no fixed term but wield the greatest power. Entitlement matters, not attendance, which is why children and the aged are citizens only in a qualified sense. The definition fits democracy best and nearly empties out in a narrow oligarchy; regime-relativity absorbs this, but makes “citizen” equivocal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a><ul><li>Entitlement to share in office is not the same as being able to exercise it.</li></ul></li></ul>","Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Hoppes describes the “process of civilization” as one that is defined by diminishing time preferences, whereby people are more and more willing to engage in capital formation and to create durable consumer goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This both explains the importance of time preferences as a means of ensuring the success of civilizations, it also explores the importance of time preferences in ensuring the success/establishment of a working civilization.</p><h4>References</h4>","Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Confucianism is built upon categorical imperatives, each depend on one’s role in society and the associated duties. There is no particular justification for the “way things are.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves as the core of Confucian philosophy, since it is what determines one’s duties.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on the <em>Book of Changes</em>, the natural order of things is found, and not invented by humans. Additionally, it does not exist for any reason. Confucius advocated for doing things because they are the right thing to do, regardless of the outcome of such actions. Often times, things do not work out, and this is attributed to <em>ming</em> (命).</p><ul><li>The idea of <em>ming</em> is that of cosmic indeterminacy and random chance, so that even if try the best that you could, things could still go wrong simply due to chance. Accepting <em>ming</em> means understanding the inevitability of the world but disregarding whether something is successful or not.</li></ul><p>From another viewpoint, in order to better the world, one does not need to be in a position of power. One needs merely to act accordingly, and through sincerely attempting to act well, one is resting in the highest good.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>These imbalances are the result of the categorical imperatives.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction\">Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction</a><ul><li>Xunzi disagrees with the formulation here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During the development of a system, it is better to have decentralization, as it allows for independence and autonomy which can aid with growth. However, when it has been developed to a certain extent, more control should be exerted over the system to ensure no unwanted/misguided growth, decay, or destabilization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important when examining the growth of states and their maintenance.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power\">The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Deliberation is defined by Aristotle as what occurs when the right course of action is not clearly defined, and reason/logic is needed to find the correct means.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding deliberation is important for understanding Aristotle’s definition of choice, which is important for his definitions of virtue and vice.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>We only deliberate about the means, and not the end, since ends are set by character and desire. So, for example, we do not deliberate “should I become more healthy?” rather, we deliberate “what should I eat today to become more healthy?” The final result of a deliberation is the “first action,” i.e., the first step in the means that eventually brings one to the end. Deliberation is also not concerned with particular facts, as such facts are the matter and concern for sense perception. For instance, we do not deliberate “Am I feeling something soft?”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end\">A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end</a><ul><li>Choice requires deliberation.</li></ul></li></ul>","Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals since they constantly side track us. A potential effect of this is our eventual dismay at the fact that we have wasted our lives pursuing distractions rather than what we actually wanted to do.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is both a topic covered in various works of literature and also a fundamental aspect of modern society, where distractions abound.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>With so many distractions in the modern world, it becomes increasingly more difficult to not only find one’s purpose, but also stick to one’s purpose without being distracted. In <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em>, Harry is constantly sidetracked by his worldly pursuits, which eventually leads to his inability to pursue his true art, writing.</p><h4>References</h4>","Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the argument that is made by Ivan Karamazov, essentially presenting the idea that because there is so much suffering in the world, the moral order that God is allowing is unacceptable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a main idea that Dostoevsky attempts to take down in his book.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In the novel, these ideas of Ivan are overridden by the philosophy of active love, and also by the fact that they are contradictory. Since Ivan states that there are no laws in the universe, then by what standard is he judging the universe of God?</p><h4>References</h4>","Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Faith is something that is generated through the lack of visible marks of providence in the world, since if such marks did exist, then faith would not be needed. It would simply be a fact of the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Bossuet’s view of the nature of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Bossuet saw all of history as being in the grasp of God, and the lack of signs of providence is not a sign that God does not exist or that providence is not real. Rather, it is this very lack of providential signs that compels a belief in God (faith).</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 205</p><p>Faithful hope does not eliminate the painful conflict between confidence and evidence; <strong>it rather enhances it</strong>. It is the same faith which creates and resolves the ultimate problem of a Christian and yet worldly existence.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a><ul><li>It seems that this is related, given that faith comes to fill the gaps where reason cannot reach.</li></ul></li></ul>","God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main way in which divine providence manifests is through the fact that although people act for and through their personal self-interest, the results of such action are all a part of God’s providence such that they result in justice and stability for societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a major way in which God affects human life and what divine providence means.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the way in which the fundamentally beastial nature of humans are tempered and made into what allows for things like justice and stability in societies is the fact that people are checked by their fear of God, or at least this was the initial impulse that allowed for this to occur. At first, God had to deceive people into believing false gods in order for the true God to eventually show himself.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a><ul><li>This note is in some ways related.</li></ul></li></ul>","Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are different conceptions of what happiness entails, and Aristotle categorizes them into three major camps: those who think that pleasure is happiness, those who think that political success/honor is happiness, and those who value contemplation as happiness. Aristotle also states that it would be “grossly disconcerting” if it were the case that happiness was “random.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle believes that true happiness can only be acquired through the final means: that is, contemplation. This forms the backbone of his philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle simple dismisses pure hedonism, presumably since it is something that does not involve “being an expert humans.” as pure hedonism is something that is achievable (at least in theory) by non-human organisms. Aristotle rejects the definition of happiness as honor, since it is too “superficial.” “Honor is felt to depend more on those who confer than on him who receives it.” Perhaps honor is something that is too passive, and is not really an <em>activity</em> of the soul. Additionally, people honor others because they are good (virtuous). This means that goodness, and not honor, is the root of happiness.</p><ul><li>I don’t know if this point is the most convincing. It seems possible that one can still be happy with the praise, not one’s intrinsic virtue. </li></ul><p>Aristotle ultimately argues that the contemplative life is one that is able to produce happiness.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>An important note to make clear here is that Aristotle’s conception of contemplation. His conception of “contemplation” is not one that promotes active discovery and curiosity towards new knowledge. Rather, it is reflecting on what one has already learned. This is not to say that the Aristotelian contemplator does not seek out new knowledge, but that he does not constantly seek out new knowledge, since that is not what brings him happiness. It is rather the process of reflecting on knowledge that has been acquired that is the basis for happiness under this conception.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s definition of the highest form of happiness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s two forms of goodness.</li></ul></li></ul>","History is inherently theological":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Meaning in history is something that can only be found through theological interpretations of history; secular history is incommensurate with theological history.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the perspective of history presents in the Bible.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This manifests through the fact that while the Bible makes a lot of claims about the coming salvation for humanity and theological events and their implications, it provides no concrete guide at all with regards to how these theological claims are to be related to secular events.</p><ul><li>When people have attempted to do this, such as Boussuet, or the Franciscans (building off of the ideas of Joachim), they have failed and demonstrated the weakness of their theology.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>It seems that this thesis cannot be held in tandem with the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>In this sense, Marxism concedes to the claim made in the current note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is constant race between education/human capital advancement and technological innovation. While human capital advancement reduces inequality (since it empowers labor), technological innovation worsens inequality (since it is capital heavy).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant since it offers an explanation for why inequality exists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The following explains the importance of education in income. </p><h4>Note</h4><p>Mankiw presses the same explanation against the rent-seeking account of the top one per cent: the earnings differential between skilled and unskilled workers over the postwar period follows the same path as the income share of the top one per cent, which is what the technology story predicts and what a rent-seeking story does not.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>Related to economic inequality, namely that it does not really harm the individual mentally.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity\">Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity</a><ul><li>The companion argument, applied to mobility rather than to the level of inequality.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While new patents have to be new and original, inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality. This leads to a system of interlocking patents, which make new innovation difficult.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial aspect of the argument that Chong sets forth in his book.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>The international-level result of this incentive, where established innovation hubs capture the gains.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li></ul>","Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Huntington claims that typically, nations Westernize first, and at the same time, they modernize, and then they reject the West.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major pattern that Huntington identifies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, we have the define the West. Huntington identifies the following as elements of the West (importantly, he says that the West is not defined by its modernization, but by its culture):</p><ul><li>It is defined by these values/institutions specifically (though this list is by no means exhaustive):<ul><li>The classical tradition</li><li>Catholicism &amp; Protestantism</li><li>Rule of Law</li><li>Separation of church and state</li><li>Representative bodies</li><li>Individualism</li><li>Social pluralism: autonomous groups beyond kinship groups that exert influence in society.</li><li>Common language: the West is influenced collectively influenced by Latin.</li></ul></li><li>Westernization first leads to modernization. After modernization, as the state gains its own power and influence, it rejects the West and embraces its indigenous culture and religion once again.</li><li>Modernization has both a societal and individual effect that contributes to cultural and religious resurgence:<ul><li><strong>Societal</strong>: modernization leads to the state becoming more powerful in terms of its industry/economy, leading the people to gain a sense of cultural assertiveness.</li><li><strong>Individual</strong>: modernization leads to <em>anomie</em>, increasingly making people turn to religion.</li></ul></li><li>Nations today are becoming less Western and more modern.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>This could be part of what non-Western nations took from the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a><ul><li>This was also adopted by the countries not in the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests\">The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests</a><ul><li>This shows Asian nations pushing back against Western institutions and ideology.</li></ul></li></ul>","Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to nonpolar molecules being hydrophobic, they are driven away from $H_{2}O$ and toward the interior of the protein, while polar and charged R groups face outward into the solvent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the principal driver of tertiary structure. The fold is largely determined by which residues must avoid water, which is also why a change in solvent, pH or salinity can unfold the protein.</p>","One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that it is important to focus on the present and not be contained by what happened in the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is both a common trope in literary works and also something that is empirically demonstrated.</p><h4>In Economics</h4><p>In economics, this manifests in the form of the concept of <em>sunk cost</em>, or costs that have already been incurred and which are irreparable. Indeed, the sunk cost fallacy is the inability to see this by operating in a manner which suggests that the costs are not actually sunk. The correct decision is to ignore sunk cost and operate on marginal costs.</p><h4>In Literature</h4><p>This is also a theme in many works of literature, for instance,</p><ul><li><em>The Sound and the Fury</em> is in large part about the failure of the Compson family to move past the Antebellum South.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Private property specifically allows for the preservation of two virtues: moderation (in women), and liberality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the arguments that Aristotle makes in favor of private property.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Private property allows for the preservation of moderation since</p><ul><li>you are especially mindful of utilizing things which are yours, and thus you will not over-indulge, and </li><li>only when there is private property can there be liberality</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems to be that this again is specific to Aristotle’s set of ethics. If it’s the case that these two moral characteristics are merely instrumental or are morally irrelevant, then this argument seems to hold very little weight.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is what the moderation point produces on an macro-scale?</li></ul></li></ul>","Regulation in favor of workers is more often just than regulation in favor of employers":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because employers are fewer, better organised and under a standing inducement to hold wages down, rules that constrain them correct an asymmetry, whereas rules that assist them compound one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This asymmetry gives a principled way to distinguish two kinds of intervention that a blanket presumption against regulation treats identically.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith states it directly: when the regulation is in support of the workman it is always just and equitable, but it is sometimes otherwise when in favour of the masters, and he describes a tacit and constant combination among employers to keep wages low.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The labor market is not perfectly competitive\">The labor market is not perfectly competitive</a><ul><li>The modern statement of the asymmetry this rests on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy\">The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy</a><ul><li>The case where the asymmetry runs the other way.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay\">Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay</a><ul><li>The competitive model that the asymmetry departs from.</li></ul></li></ul>","SNAP does not cause economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>SNAP does not boost GDP as although it gives people more money to spend, this can, at best, lead to a temporary spike in GDP, but it cannot lead to sustained growth of the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fallacious argument that many discuss regarding the efficacy of food stamps.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>SNAP redistributes wealth from the relative more wealth to the less wealthy, and since poorer people have a higher MPC, this would naturally lead to a temporary boost in GDP. However, a dollar spent is a dollar not saved, and, without the savings that would otherwise have existed, long-term economic growth is stunted.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not drive up food prices\">SNAP does not drive up food prices</a></li></ul>","Social Security contributes to the federal debt":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Social Security program, unlike what some claim, does indeed contribute to the overall federal debt, as since 2010, it has run a deficit, meaning that the amount it receives in payroll taxes is less than the amount that the program spends on benefits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the great necessity for reform in the Social Security system, as over the next decade, Social Security could contribute 4.1 trillion to the overall federal debt.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Often, it is explained that Social Security has not contributed to the debt because they ran surplus in the past, the products of which is stored as the Social Security Trust Fund. The idea is that Social Security could dip into these funds when they run deficits, and thus, not add to the deficit. However, view is facile for several reasons:</p><ol><li>The trust fund is expected to run out by the early 2030s.</li><li>The trust fund is not actually consisting of liquid assets. Rather, it is made up of special-issue Treasury bonds, which means that when Social Security needs to withdraw some amount from the trust fund, the federal government actually needs to find the money to for the trust fund through either tax hikes or increases to the federal deficit.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings\">Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings</a><ul><li>The distributive structure that produces the deficit described here, since the taxable ceiling caps revenue.</li></ul></li><li><em>How to Cure Health Care</em></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li></ul>","The Partition of Jin ended the Spring and Autumn Period":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Spring and Autumn period was ended with the partition of the Jin, as the three dukes that ruled under the Jin gained enough power as to establish kingdoms for themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This triggered the beginning of the Warring States period, during which there was large scale warfare, as states began to more openly fight against each other.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a></li></ul>","The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US is made weaker by embracing multiculturalism, which undermines the core Western civilization that underlies the US. Ultimately, this makes the US directionless and weak.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents an argument against multiculturalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The US should instead embrace its civilizational roots and reconnect with Europe through things like free trade agreements in order to revitalize Western civilization. Specifically, Huntington states that the US must:</p><ul><li>Integrate militarily, politically, and economically with other states of the West.</li><li>Incorporate into NATO &amp; EU more Western (culturally speaking) states in Europe.</li><li>Encourage alignment of Latin American states with the West.</li><li>Restrict the military development of Islamic and Sinic states.</li><li>Slow the drift of Japan away from the West.</li><li>Accept Russia as the core state of Orthodoxy.</li><li>Maintain Western technological and military superiority.</li></ul><p>Most importantly, the US must <strong>recognize that Western intervention in the affairs of other civilizations is counterproductive and almost always detrimental to US interests in the long run.</strong></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a><ul><li>Huntington argues that this gap needs to be closed to ensure civilizational stability.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>Due to the inherent differences in culture with respect to the way that different cultures view politics, US intervention in places across the globe that have different cultures then is necessarily counterproductive.</li></ul></li></ul>","The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If the first derivative of a function is equal to zero, test for the values around the point at which it is equal to zero. If the values left are positive and the right, negative, then the function has a relative max at that point. If the opposite, there is a relative min at that value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The first derivative test is one of the fundamental test in calculus that is used to determine the behavior of a function at a specified location.</p><h4>Mathematical Representation</h4><p>Given that $f'(c)=0$ and the first derivative of $f$ exists on an open interval containing $c$.</p><ol><li>If $\\lim_{ x \\to c^- }f'(x)&gt;0$ and $\\lim_{ x \\to c^+ }f'(x)&lt;0$,, then $f(c)$ is a relative maximum.</li><li>If $\\lim_{ x \\to c^- }f'(x)&lt;0$ and $\\lim_{ x \\to c^+ }f'(x)&gt;0$ then $f(c)$ is a relative minimum.</li><li>Otherwise, there is no local maxima or minima.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","The match between a task and whoever performs it best is intuitively primitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The judgment that the person best able to do a job should be the one who does it appears to be basic rather than derived, arising in the way an aesthetic judgment does, without an argument standing behind it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If the intuition is genuinely primitive, then arguments against meritocracy have to explain the intuition away rather than merely outweigh it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The example usually given is deliberately trivial. When the goal is to produce bagels, it seems immediately true that the job should go to whoever makes the best bagels, and no further premise is doing work. The obvious reply is that the intuition tracks the consequentialist point about output and is not about desert at all.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics\">A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics</a><ul><li>The methodological principle that gives such intuitions evidential weight.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>The ambiguity this intuition trades on.</li></ul></li></ul>","The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The most important contribution that the Western made to Chinese philosophy was the introduction of methods of positive logical analysis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Chinese philosophy was largely focused on negative analysis (what something is not), rather than positive analysis (what something is). Therefore, the West made a crucial contribution to Chinese philosophy in this way, but introducing this method.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Ultimately, Fong Youlan argues that the future of Chinese philosophy (specifically metaphysics) lies in a combination of the use of the negative and the positive methods.</p><ul><li>He argues that a perfect philosophical method should start with the positive method and end with the negative method in order to achieve the best results.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a><ul><li>In this way, Chinese philosophy also has something to contribute to the philosophy of the future, both with its focus on the negative method and also its focus on spiritual elevation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>One part of this means a greater focus on the idea of finding out the causes, rather than just doing what works.</li></ul></li></ul>","To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for one to be able to reach enlightenment, it is actually quite simple. One simply needs to act in the way required of them (仁，孝，礼， etc.), and also do so with sincerity (诚).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Confucian path to enlightenment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the average person, this means beginning with education, and learning through practice, and eventually coming to be able to understand the “realness” of the concepts (achieve sincerity).</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Zhongyong</em> 21</p><p>自诚明,谓之性;自明诚,谓之教。诚则明矣,明则诚矣。</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>This is the reason that they have such a strong focus on education.</li></ul></li></ul>","Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Standard Medicare has a fee-for-service system, meaning that physicians are compensated for the quantity of service that they provide, not necessarily the quality of the service that they provide.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the incentives of doctors can be distorted, causing them to provide services that are not necessarily needed, and thus, drive up the cost of Medicare. This may in part explain my American healthcare is so expensive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>The effect of the contents of this note on the federal debt are noted here.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
